import{_ as e,o as a,c as r,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.BJ2Tlia3.js";const o="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l1_gate.Ch8MqrXS.svg",n="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l2_gate.TiwOr0Me.svg",d="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l3_gate.DaRisD-G.svg",i="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_lapack_gate.DroNuubM.svg",l="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l1.CgInp2lD.svg",c="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l2.DfBHf6Pd.svg",h="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l3.qh1EJhG3.svg",g="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_lapack.D8cLcw4Y.svg",p="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l1_accelerate.DDSKUpbm.svg",m="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l2_accelerate.0HvrUI4N.svg",y="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_l3_accelerate.DSMhtH2h.svg",u="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/assets/perf_lapack_accelerate.MADx4Z8y.svg",S=JSON.parse('{"title":"Apple Silicon","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"performance_apple.md","filePath":"performance_apple.md","lastUpdated":null}'),x={name:"performance_apple.md"};function b(f,t,w,L,A,v){return a(),r("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="Apple-Silicon" tabindex="-1">Apple Silicon <a class="header-anchor" href="#Apple-Silicon" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Apple Silicon {#Apple-Silicon}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Results on Apple Silicon (arm64), comparing PureBLAS against <strong>OpenBLAS</strong> and <strong>Accelerate</strong> (Apple&#39;s own vecLib BLAS/LAPACK, forwarded via <code>libblastrampoline</code>&#39;s ILP64 <code>$NEWLAPACK$ILP64</code> interface — see <a href="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/methodology#accelerate-on-apple-silicon">Methodology</a>). This architecture is not part of the <a href="./performance">main fleet</a>, which stays AMD-only.</p><p><strong>SME</strong>, the Scalable Matrix Extension, is a published Arm extension implemented on M4 and later. On this machine it carries Float64 <code>gemm</code>, <code>gemv</code> in both the untransposed and transposed forms, and — through them — <code>symm</code>, <code>syrk</code>, <code>syr2k</code>, <code>trmm</code>, <code>trsm</code>, <code>trmv</code>, <code>symv</code> and the LAPACK factorizations. Float32 <code>gemm</code> has a second route to the unit, gated on its own feature constant, its own register tile and its own published entry pointer: the 32-bit outer product is base SME where the 64-bit one is an extension, so neither precision implies the other and neither arm may stand in for the other. Anything the eligibility predicates decline runs on NEON, whose Float64 vector is 2 lanes wide. Those two paths differ by roughly an order of magnitude, so the ratios below split by size rather than averaging into one figure.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong> BLAS-1, BLAS-2, BLAS-3 and the four core LAPACK factorizations (<code>potrf</code>, <code>getrf</code>, <code>geqrf</code>, <code>gesvd</code>), Float64 only. Complex, ForwardDiff-dual and the rest of LAPACK are not covered here.</p><p><strong>Caveat that does not apply to the AMD fleet:</strong> macOS has no equivalent of the Linux <code>cpufreq</code>/<code>taskset</code> locking <a href="./methodology">Methodology</a> treats as mandatory for a gate-quality measurement. These numbers are <strong>not frequency-locked</strong> — read them as directional, not gate verdicts. Measured round-to-round spread on this box is nonetheless small: across eight rounds the q10-q90 band is 0.04% on the anchor workload and 0.3-1.7% on <code>gemm</code> cells (<code>bench/probes/apple_unlocked_spread.jl</code>). That is the spread of ROUND MEDIANS; the spread of individual samples inside a round is far wider in the small BLAS-3 cells — 1.1-1.2x at the binding <code>syrk</code> and <code>trmm</code> sizes, and 2.15x in Accelerate&#39;s <code>gemm</code> at n = 1024, which is why that one cell is not a usable figure.</p><p><strong>Machine:</strong> Apple M6 (<code>_vwidth(Float64)=2</code>, <code>sme_max_svl_b=64</code> ⇒ 8 FP64 lanes in a ZA tile), unlocked clock. The PureBLAS arm of every cell was measured at commit <code>076b102a</code> on 2026-11-09; the OpenBLAS and Accelerate arms are reused from runs between 2026-09-22 and 2026-09-28. Full provenance: <a href="./assets/apple/provenance"><code>provenance.md</code></a>.</p><p>⚠ <strong>The two arms of this cache are six weeks apart, and the box moved between them.</strong> Across the 591 cells the two PureBLAS runs share, PureBLAS absolute times are <strong>5.3% higher</strong> in the later run; the anchor workload — a fixed kernel measured once per run, independent of any op here — reads 5-6% higher by the same comparison. So the near-uniform fall in every ratio below, about 9% on the median op against <em>both</em> references at once, is machine state rather than code. 75 of 281 comparison cells carry arm anchors more than 5% apart (worst 5.8%, <code>bench/check_arm_anchors.sh</code>), and against the gate&#39;s own 2% tolerance 15 ops have a binding cell that is not adjudicable at all. <strong>Read any single ratio here as carrying a 5-10% machine-state term</strong>; only a run that measures all three arms in one pass removes it. The box also has no frequency pin, so <code>bench/check_arm_clocks.sh</code> has nothing to compare against and <code>bench/check_cache_busy.sh</code> has no busy field to read — on macOS <code>plots.jl</code>&#39;s contention guard needs <code>/proc</code> and skips.</p><p>Six ops moved further than that term, measured PureBLAS-against-PureBLAS with no reference involved: <code>iamax</code> is 60% slower than in September, <code>trmv</code> and <code>pstrfU</code> 25%, <code>symm</code>, <code>potrsL</code> and <code>potri</code> 20%, against a 5.3% median. <code>nrm2</code> is 6% faster. Those are regression candidates, not confirmed regressions: two foreign processes held about one core busy throughout the 2026-11-09 run, so a quiet-box re-measure is what would settle them.</p><h2 id="Headline" tabindex="-1">Headline <a class="header-anchor" href="#Headline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Headline {#Headline}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The median and the worst cell tell opposite stories here, and the gate reports the worst cell.</strong> On the median PureBLAS beats Accelerate on <code>nrm2</code> (2.09x), <code>symv</code> (1.71x), <code>gesvd</code> (1.24x), <code>iamax</code> (1.13x), <code>getrf</code> (1.11x), <code>axpy</code> (1.04x) and <code>spmv</code> (1.02x), ties it on <code>dot</code>, <code>asum</code> and <code>trsvLT</code>, and beats OpenBLAS on the median of every BLAS-3 and LAPACK op measured. Only <code>nrm2</code> passes the gate; every other op has at least one size where it does not.</p><p>Where the remaining gap lives, by op family:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">family</th><th style="text-align:right;">shape of the gap</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">BLAS-3 <code>gemm</code>-fed</td><td style="text-align:right;">parity at n = 2048-2100; the loss is concentrated at n ≤ 1000, and n = 4096 reads 0.76</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>syrk</code> / <code>syr2k</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the largest open gap: <code>syrk</code> is 0.14 at n = 50 and 0.47 on the median</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trmm</code> / <code>trmmR</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">both bottom at n = 100 — 0.16 side L, 0.14 side R — on medians of 0.32 and 0.39</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">BLAS-2</td><td style="text-align:right;">closed at mid and large n; <code>gemvT</code>, <code>trmv</code> dip where their routing cuts sit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">BLAS-1</td><td style="text-align:right;">parity with OpenBLAS; Accelerate leads at the largest sizes, where DRAM binds</td></tr></tbody></table><p><code>gemm</code> against Accelerate across the ladder:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">n</th><th style="text-align:right;">8</th><th style="text-align:right;">32</th><th style="text-align:right;">50</th><th style="text-align:right;">100</th><th style="text-align:right;">128</th><th style="text-align:right;">256</th><th style="text-align:right;">512</th><th style="text-align:right;">1000</th><th style="text-align:right;">2048</th><th style="text-align:right;">2100</th><th style="text-align:right;">4096</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">ratio</td><td style="text-align:right;">2.21</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.96</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.79</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>0.69</strong></td><td style="text-align:right;">0.88</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.88</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.93</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.83</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.03</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.02</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.76</td></tr></tbody></table><p>n = 100 is the binding cell and is what the <code>gemm</code> gate figure reports. n = 1024 reads 1.67, but Accelerate&#39;s own q10-q90 spread in that one cell is 2.15x, so it is not a usable number — the cells either side of it read 0.83 and 1.03, and both sit under a 1.02 spread. <strong>n = 4096 fell from 1.10 to 0.76 between the two PureBLAS runs</strong>, and the cause is the box, not the code: the 2026-09 source (<code>52332de7</code>) and the current one, run alternately on the same box the same day, differ by 3% at n = 4096 and 1% at n = 100, while both take 0.39-0.43 s at n = 4096 against 0.27 s in the September cache. Treat the n = 4096 ratio as invalid until a quiet-box re-measure.</p><h2 id="What-reaches-SME,-and-when" tabindex="-1">What reaches SME, and when <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-reaches-SME,-and-when" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What reaches SME, and when {#What-reaches-SME,-and-when}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Eligibility is governed by TILE OCCUPANCY, not size alone, and the two floors differ because the costs differ. A shape that divides the 16-row panel exactly pays no remainder panel at all and qualifies from <strong>two whole tiles, n = 32</strong>. A shape with a remainder must also amortize the padded block, and qualifies from <strong>n = 48</strong>. So <code>_sme_tile_ok(32,32)</code> and <code>(48,48)</code> and <code>(49,49)</code> are true while <code>(33,33)</code> and <code>(40,40)</code> are false; n = 33 and n = 40 measure 0.66x and 0.93x of the NEON path they stay on (those two figures come from the earlier probe run and were not re-measured on 2026-11-09).</p><p>The Float32 twin <code>_sme_tile_ok32</code> admits <strong>one</strong> whole register tile each way where the Float64 path requires two, over a 32-row tile rather than a 16-row one: it takes <code>min(m,n) ≥ 32</code>, passes any shape dividing 32 exactly, and otherwise asks <code>max(m,n) ≥ 96</code>. Which multiple that ragged floor lands on is measured, not inherited from the Float64 arm. Every ratio on this page is Float64, so none of them exercises it.</p><p>The routines that reach the unit through <code>gemm</code> do so by declining their own private packed paths: <code>syrk</code> and <code>syr2k</code> both hand off from n = 96, and <code>trmm</code> side-R declines its packed kernel wherever the panel gemms it would replace are themselves eligible — the predicate asks <code>_sme_eligible</code> with those gemms&#39; own operands and shape rather than restating part of it. Above k = 1792 that packed kernel is also what makes a threaded call bit-reproducible against a serial one, because the flat panel loop keys its kernel choice on the band&#39;s own row count; so the same predicate declines the thread pool, and a banded flat loop is never reachable. The side-R recursion bottoms out two SME tiles down rather than at <code>_L3_NB</code>, and <code>trmm!</code>&#39;s tiny fast path carries the same base so the two cannot disagree about which calls it may answer directly.</p><p><code>gemv</code> reaches SME in both forms, with floors that are not the same quantity: the untransposed form asks for <code>m·n</code> work (half of L1 in elements), the transposed form asks for <strong>m alone</strong> (256 rows), because its per-column-group fold is O(1) against O(m) of streamed column and does not care how many columns follow. <code>trmv</code> hands its off-diagonal scatter to <code>gemv</code> from n = 512; <code>symv</code> splits its off-diagonal into a <code>gemv</code>-N and a <code>gemv</code>-T from n = 384, reading that block twice in exchange for the bandwidth.</p><h2 id="Two-harness-properties-this-page-depends-on" tabindex="-1">Two harness properties this page depends on <a class="header-anchor" href="#Two-harness-properties-this-page-depends-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two harness properties this page depends on {#Two-harness-properties-this-page-depends-on}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Both were defects once; both are now part of how the numbers are produced, and a reproduction that skips either will not match.</p><p><strong>BLAS-1 at large n is measured <code>cold</code>.</strong> <code>bench/plots.jl</code>&#39;s <code>_L1REP(s) = clamp(8_000_000 ÷ s, 30, 20000)</code> amortizes per-call timer overhead by running <code>reps</code> calls back-to-back <strong>on the same operand buffer</strong> inside one timed window. At n=1e6 that buffer is 16 MB — small enough to stay resident in L2/SLC across all 30 calls, so the window measures repeated access to a warm buffer rather than DRAM streaming. With genuinely cold, freshly allocated arrays far too large for any cache (10M-50M elements, 240 MB-1.2 GB), Accelerate&#39;s real <code>axpy</code> advantage over OpenBLAS is <strong>1.06-1.36x</strong> — a modest per-core DRAM-bandwidth edge, not the ~4.3x the reps-amortized form implies. The <code>cold</code> flag forces <code>reps=1</code> and is used for every L1 figure on this page. It is opt-in and changes nothing for the AMD fleet. <strong>Open question:</strong> whether the same artifact affects the AMD fleet&#39;s own large-n L1 cells — Zen3&#39;s 32 MiB L3 also holds a 16 MB working set — diagnosed here, unconfirmed on x86.</p><p><strong>Accelerate is held to one thread by an environment variable, not by LBT.</strong> <code>LinearAlgebra.BLAS.set_num_threads(1)</code> constrains OpenBLAS, AOCL and MKL. It does <strong>not</strong> constrain vecLib, which reads <code>VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS</code> at its own first use, independently of LBT&#39;s knob. Left unset, a <code>dgemm</code> at n=4000 pins process CPU at a sustained ~190-200% despite the call. <code>plots.jl</code> therefore sets <code>ENV[&quot;VECLIB_MAXIMUM_THREADS&quot;]</code> at load time, before Accelerate can be forwarded; the same workload then holds ~99-100%. The single-thread <code>dgemm</code> figure this yields is ~469 GFLOP/s — the second thread buys only ~16%, consistent with SME being one unit per cluster rather than a per-core datapath. LAPACK is unaffected: <code>potrf</code> at n=2048 sampled clean single-threaded either way, so vecLib appears to auto-parallelize only certain BLAS-3 routines at this size.</p><h2 id="Tuning:-tune!-and-its-limits" tabindex="-1">Tuning: <code>tune!()</code> and its limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#Tuning:-tune!-and-its-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tuning: `tune!()` and its limits {#Tuning:-tune!-and-its-limits}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>PureBLAS.tune!(dryrun=true, unlocked=true)</code> — the project&#39;s Measure-tier auto-calibrator, see <code>src/tune.jl</code> — found two unpinned wins on this box when it was last run: <code>gemvT</code> routing switching to per-column mode at n≥2048 for +11-13%, and <code>potrf</code>&#39;s upper-direct cutoff moving from 20 to 8 for +10-11%. <strong>The <code>gemvT</code> finding predates the SME transposed path and has not been re-run against it</strong>, so treat it as unverified for the current tree; the <code>potrf</code> one is independent of that path. Everything else it checks — gemv tile shape, <code>ger</code> panel width, <code>sytrf</code>, banded LU — the shipped default already wins or it is a wash.</p><p><strong><code>tune!()</code> does not reach the thing driving the broad L3 gap.</strong> <code>gemm</code>&#39;s microkernel tile shape (<code>_MR</code>x<code>_NR</code>) is governed by <code>_ILP_TARGET=16</code>, a <em>Derive-tier</em> formula carrying an explicit x86 FMA-latency/port assumption, applied here unvalidated. There is no calibrator for it on any architecture. Closing it needs either an ARM-specific derivation or a new Measure-tier calibrator — separate, larger work than a data-generation pass.</p><p>⚠ <strong>A routing cut is only as current as its slower arm.</strong> Several cuts on this page choose between a private kernel and a route that reaches SME, and every one of them is a frozen comparison: when the <code>gemm</code> under it changes, the cut keeps steering by the old answer and nothing in the build notices. <code>bench/probes/sme_syr2k_route_recheck.jl</code> and <code>bench/probes/sme_min_crossover.jl</code> re-derive the two that matter most; re-run them whenever the kernel moves.</p><h2 id="vs-OpenBLAS-and-Accelerate,-per-op" tabindex="-1">vs OpenBLAS and Accelerate, per op <a class="header-anchor" href="#vs-OpenBLAS-and-Accelerate,-per-op" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;vs OpenBLAS and Accelerate, per op {#vs-OpenBLAS-and-Accelerate,-per-op}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ratio is PB / reference, <strong>median (worst cell)</strong> across the measured size ladder — BLAS-2 and BLAS-3 reach n = 4096, LAPACK caps at n = 2048, and BLAS-1 runs the full 1e3..1e6 measured <code>cold</code> (see above). Gate is PB / max(OpenBLAS, Accelerate) — the same two-significant-digit rounding rule as the <a href="/PureBLAS.jl/previews/PR98/methodology#the-gate">main fleet</a> (<code>bench/gatecrit.jl</code>). The gate is a <strong>worst-cell</strong> figure, so a FAIL can still have a winning median — true below for every op except <code>asum</code>, <code>scal</code> and <code>gbmvN</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">level</th><th style="text-align:right;">op</th><th style="text-align:right;">vs OpenBLAS</th><th style="text-align:right;">vs Accelerate</th><th style="text-align:right;">gate</th><th style="text-align:right;">verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>axpy</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00 (0.97)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.04</strong> (0.65)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.648</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>dot</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.07</strong> (1.00)</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00 (0.96)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.961</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>nrm2</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>8.83</strong> (7.00)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.09</strong> (1.67)</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.672</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>PASS</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>asum</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00 (0.96)</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00 (0.51)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.506</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>scal</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00 (0.92)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.85 (0.38)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.377</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L1</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>iamax</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">0.61 (0.50)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.13</strong> (0.99)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.503</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemvN</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>6.41</strong> (1.73)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.78 (0.49)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.489</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemvT</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.01</strong> (1.47)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.83 (0.32)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.318</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>ger</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.34</strong> (0.91)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.87 (0.58)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.579</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>symv</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.38</strong> (0.66)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.71</strong> (0.83)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.660</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trmv</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.84</strong> (1.51)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.62 (0.36)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.359</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trsv</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.44</strong> (1.23)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.80 (0.65)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.652</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trsvLN</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.35</strong> (1.19)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.79 (0.65)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.653</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trsvLT</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.05</strong> (1.39)</td><td style="text-align:right;">1.00 (0.79)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.793</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>spmv</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.44</strong> (1.03)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.02</strong> (0.62)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.615</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gbmvN</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">0.79 (0.74)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.73 (0.65)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.649</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L2</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>sbmv</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.79</strong> (2.73)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.71 (0.62)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.619</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gemm</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>6.28</strong> (2.18)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.90 (0.69)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.688</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>symm</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>3.15</strong> (1.03)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.56 (0.42)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.417</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>syrk</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>3.50</strong> (0.77)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.47 (0.14)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.140</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>syr2k</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>4.19</strong> (1.27)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.63 (0.33)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.327</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trmm</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.40</strong> (0.54)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.32 (0.16)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.156</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trmmR</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.27</strong> (0.50)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.39 (0.14)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.142</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trsm</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.04</strong> (1.03)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.50 (0.32)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.321</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">L3</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>trsmR</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>2.45</strong> (1.22)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.56 (0.43)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.431</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LP</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>potrf</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.97</strong> (1.53)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.55 (0.36)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.358</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LP</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>getrf</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.90</strong> (0.89)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.11</strong> (0.52)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.515</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LP</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>geqrf</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.21</strong> (1.13)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.98 (0.26)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.264</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">LP</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>gesvd</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.89</strong> (1.02)</td><td style="text-align:right;"><strong>1.24</strong> (0.88)</td><td style="text-align:right;">0.878</td><td style="text-align:right;">FAIL</td></tr></tbody></table><p>203 of 281 measured cells sit below the gate. The worst are <code>syrk</code> at n = 32-100, <code>trmmR</code> at n = 50-100 and <code>trmm</code> at n = 50-128. The two binding cells carry a q10-q90 sample spread of 1.15x (<code>syrk</code> at n = 50) and 1.13x (<code>trmmR</code> at n = 100), with their Accelerate arms at 1.14x and 1.10x, so a tuning change worth less than about 15% in those cells is not separable from round-to-round noise in this cache.</p><p>Full machine-readable tables: <a href="./assets/apple/gen_table"><code>gen_table.md</code></a> (vs OpenBLAS), <a href="./assets/apple/gen_table_accelerate"><code>gen_table_accelerate.md</code></a> (vs Accelerate).</p><h2 id="Plots" tabindex="-1">Plots <a class="header-anchor" href="#Plots" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Plots {#Plots}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Ratio-vs-size, same style as the <a href="./performance">main fleet pages</a> (dashed line = parity, band = q10-q90 spread). &quot;gate&quot; divides by whichever reference is faster at each point.</p><p><img src="'+o+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+n+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+d+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+i+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+l+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+c+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+h+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+g+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+p+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+m+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+y+'" alt=""></p><p><img src="'+u+`" alt=""></p><h2 id="Reproduce" tabindex="-1">Reproduce <a class="header-anchor" href="#Reproduce" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reproduce {#Reproduce}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench group=L1 arms=pb,openblas,accelerate cold nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench group=L2 arms=pb,openblas,accelerate nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench group=L3 arms=pb,openblas,accelerate nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench op=potrf arms=pb,openblas,accelerate maxsize=2048 nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench op=getrf arms=pb,openblas,accelerate maxsize=2048 nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench op=geqrf arms=pb,openblas,accelerate maxsize=2048 nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench op=gesvd arms=pb,openblas,accelerate maxsize=2048 nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl outdir=docs/src/assets/apple</span></span></code></pre></div><p>That is the full-arm form, and it is the only one whose ratios are free of the machine-state term above. The 2026-11-09 figures came from a PureBLAS-only refresh over the September reference arms, one launch per group because <code>group=</code> is an exact match and a comma list matches nothing:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>for g in L1 L2 L3 LP CL1 CL2 CL3 CLP DL1 DL2 DL3 DLP; do</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl bench group=$g arms=pb cold nodraw</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>done</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>julia --project=bench bench/plots.jl outdir=docs/src/assets/apple</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>group=</code> and <code>op=</code> runs MERGE per arm per cell; a <code>bench</code> run without either REPLACES the cache whole, so the same command without <code>group=</code> would drop both reference arms and <code>plots.jl</code> refuses it. <code>outdir=</code> is not optional here: the default render draws every host cache on disk into <code>docs/src/assets</code>, and this box holds only the Apple cache, so a bare <code>bench/publish.sh</code> would overwrite the fleet figures and drop three microarchitectures.</p><p><code>bench/Project.toml</code> is the one bench environment on every box, Apple included. Accelerate needs no package at all — it is an OS framework, forwarded by raw dylib path exactly like OpenBLAS — and <code>AOCL_jll</code> resolves everywhere, reporting <code>is_available() == false</code> where AMD ships no artifact, so the <code>aocl</code> arm simply is not offered on Apple silicon.</p>`,53)])])}const k=e(x,[["render",b]]);export{S as __pageData,k as default};
